uthor, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Mathematics > Differential Geometry\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 11 Mar 2024 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 13 Oct 2025 (this version, v3)]\nL9: # Title:Isoperimetric Inequalities on Slabs with applications to Cubes and Gaussian Slabs\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Emanuel Milman L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Isoperimetric Inequalities on Slabs with applications to Cubes and Gaussian Slabs, by Emanuel Milman\nL14: \nL15: cite7†View PDF cite8†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:We study isoperimetric inequalities on \"slabs\", namely weighted Riemannian manifolds obtained as the product of the uniform measure on a finite length interval with a codimension-one base. As our two main applications, we consider the case when the base is the flat torus $\\mathbb{R}^2 / 2 \\mathbb{Z}^2$ and the standard Gaussian measure in $\\mathbb{R}^{n-1}$.\nL17: > The isoperimetric conjecture on the three-dimensional cube predicts that minimizers are enclosed by spheres about a corner, cylinders about an edge and coordinate planes. This has only been established for relative volumes close to $0$, $1/2$ and $1$ by compactness arguments. Our analysis confirms the isoperimetric conjecture on the three-dimensional cube with side lengths $(\\beta,1,1)$ in a new range of relatives volumes $\\bar v \\in [0,1/2]$.\nL18: In particular, we confirm the conjecture for the standard cube ($\\beta=1$) for all $\\bar v \\leq 0.120582$, when $\\beta \\leq 0.919431$ for the entire range where spheres are conjectured to be minimizing, and also for all $\\bar v \\in [0,1/2] \\setminus (\\frac{1}{\\pi} - \\frac{\\beta}{4},\\frac{1}{\\pi} + \\frac{\\beta}{4})$.\nL19: When $\\beta \\leq 0.919431$ we reduce the validity of the full conjecture to establishing that the half-plane $\\{ x \\in [0,\\beta] \\times [0,1]^2 \\; ; \\; x_3 \\leq \\frac{1}{\\pi} \\}$ is an isoperimetric minimizer. We also show that the analogous conjecture on a high-dimensional cube $[0,1]^n$ is false for $n \\geq 10$.\nL20: > In the case of a slab with a Gaussian base of width $T>0$, we identify a phase transition when $T = \\sqrt{2 \\pi}$ and when $T = \\pi$. In particular, while products of half-planes with $[0,T]$ are always minimizing when $T \\leq \\sqrt{2 \\pi}$, when $T > \\pi$ they are never minimizing, being beaten by Gaussian unduloids. In the range $T \\in (\\sqrt{2 \\pi},\\pi]$, a potential trichotomy occurs.\nL21: Comments:  | 63 pages, 9 figures. Added references, improved Introduction, repeated numerical verification using FLINT. To appear in Comm. Pure Appl. Math. (CPAM)\nL22: Subjects:  | Differential Geometry (math.DG); Functional Analysis (math.FA); Metric Geometry (math.MG)\nL23: Cite as:  | cite9†arXiv:2403.06602 [math.DG]\nL24:    | (or cite10†arXiv:2403.06602v3 [math.DG] for this version)\nL25:    | cite11†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2403.06602†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL26: ## Submission history\nL27: \nL28: From: Emanuel Milman [cite12†view email ]\nL29: cite5†[v1] Mon, 11 Mar 2024 10:49:17 UTC (1,037 KB)\nL30: cite13†[v2] Mon, 18 Mar 2024 13:15:00 UTC (1,207 KB)\nL31: [v3] Mon, 13 Oct 2025 14:09:34 UTC (1,209 KB)\nL32: \nL33: Full-text links:\nL34: \nL35: ## Access Paper:\nL36: \nL37: View a PDF of the paper titled Isoperimetric Inequalities on Slabs with applications to Cubes and Gaussian Slabs, by Emanuel Milman\nL38: \nL39:   * cite7†View PDF L40:   * cite8†HTML (experimental) L41:   * cite14†TeX Source L42: \nL43: cite15†view license L44: ### Current browse context:\nL45: \nL46: math.DG\nL47: \nL48: cite16†< prev |   cite17†next > L49: \nL50: cite18†new | cite19†recent | cite20†2024-03 L51: \nL52: Change to browse by:\nL53: \nL54: cite21†math L55: cite22†math.FA L56: cite23†math.MG L57: \nL58: ### References & Citations\nL59: \nL60:   * cite24†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.ed